;; A WORKED EXAMPLE shipped with the KIT — not a configuration. `bb init` copies the one
;; matching your seat (`--seat <name>`, default claude) to <name>-build/profile.edn; edit it there.
;;
;; SEAT :claude. Its sibling is agy-ide.edn, and the pair is instructive: they
;; were near mirror images: here Anthropic wrote and Google and OpenAI verified;
;; there Google writes and Anthropic verifies. Since 2026-10-16 Google writes
;; here too, on a measurement (the Coder's comment), and Anthropic and OpenAI
;; verify. In both, an Anthropic role goes
;; direct to Anthropic's API on the :anthropic shape, and every other role over
;; OpenRouter on the :openai shape - so each file shows both tool-call shapes.
;;
;; Model names are dated selections (decision log P0-3: "Specific models are
;; dated selections"). The SHAPE is the part meant to outlive them. The
;; selections below were made by bake-offs during the KIT's development; their
;; records are not in this repository, so what each rests on is said here in
;; words and not in figures. `bb bake-off` is how to make your own.
;; The ROUTE of the Anthropic roles is a dated decision too: direct since
;; 2026-10-16, the person's decision (`NOTES.md` row 128); over OpenRouter pinned
;; to `anthropic` from 2026-09-25 until then (row 68), from a paid probe of
;; 2026-09-18 (row 25).
;;
;; Three things this file is careful about:
;;
;;   ONE SEAT. The interactive client is where the human sits. The dispatched
;;   roles are reached over HTTP by the harness and are not anyone's editor —
;;   so there is exactly one :seat, and it is a scalar so it cannot grow a
;;   second. :seat names a tool `bb doctor` knows how to probe, which is why it
;;   is :claude rather than :claude-code — doctor probes binaries.
;;
;;   THE CODER'S FAMILY FOLLOWS THE SEAT, by convention and not by rule. You
;;   are already being helped by that family interactively, so letting it write
;;   the code too keeps one story; what must then differ is the VERIFIERS. It
;;   is not checked, because OpenCode 2 and Pi are bring-your-own-model clients
;;   with no inherent family — the relation is not expressible for half the
;;   seats this kit supports. THIS FILE SETS IT ASIDE since 2026-10-16: a
;;   measurement put another family's Coder ahead (the Coder's comment), and a
;;   convention gives way to a measurement.
;;
;;   VERIFIER ≠ CODER FAMILY. Method §05 and decision log P0-3, and this one IS
;;   checked. The Tester and the code reviewer both verify the Coder, so neither may
;;   share its family, nor each other's. `bb profile` is the only place that
;;   rule has ever been anything but prose.
;;
;;   NO KEYS, EVER. :key-env NAMES an environment variable. This file is
;;   committed; a field that could hold a secret would eventually be handed one.
;;
;; THE ANTHROPIC ROLES GO DIRECT, and four things the money tooling did on
;; OpenRouter's side are given up for them - known, and taken:
;;
;;   TWO KEYS, ONE BALANCE. `bb balance`, and the two lines `run-loop run`
;;   prints around the loop, read OpenRouter's key limit and account credit
;;   from its API. Anthropic's API has no balance endpoint: a direct role's
;;   money is only ever SPEND, summed from the records after the fact, and
;;   the balance line prints it as Anthropic spend.
;;
;;   THE COST IS COMPUTED, NOT REPORTED. A direct completion carries no cost:
;;   the record's is usage × the role's :pricing, marked ~ and only as fresh
;;   as its :as-of. The arithmetic was checked on a paid probe, 2026-10-16 - a
;;   cache write and a cache read among its four requests. When Anthropic's
;;   list prices move, the :pricing here moves with them.
;;
;;   TWO CREDIT STOPS. A 402 stops the loop by name (:credit, owner human)
;;   with the endpoint and the key variable, not as one more failed dispatch -
;;   on either key now, so check both before a run against the plan's per-run
;;   floor (§13); the first adopter was stopped once on EACH provider's
;;   credit, mid-run, with nothing having warned.
;;
;;   NO GENERATION RECORD for a direct role. OpenRouter's says who served a
;;   completion, at which service tier, with native token counts; direct, the
;;   endpoint is the provider ("Anthropic API") and the tokens are the
;;   response's own. It also spares the fixed ~9 s wait at the end of every
;;   dispatch for the last completion's record (row 25).
;;
;; THE ROUNDS A DISPATCH MAY TAKE are this project's to set, per role, as
;; `:max-rounds n` in a role block below. A round is one completion answering
;; the last tool result; the Tester, writing a test per target against a stub
;; it cannot run, takes more than the Coder. The Tester's is set here, 40, as
;; the first real project set it on 2026-10-02 after its Tester ended on the
;; harness's default of 24 three times on a contract of fifty targets; with
;; packets of ten to twenty targets after, no Tester reached 40 and the Coder
;; never passed 16 of its 24. The number is still a project's to measure: the
;; report's "Dispatch rounds" line says how close each dispatch came.

{:seat :claude

 :roles
 {;; CODER — Gemini 4 Argon at medium effort, over OpenRouter pinned to Google
  ;; Vertex. A dated selection of 2026-10-16 (plan 6.5, P-18), the person's
  ;; decision on a measurement: packets of the first KIT project's first stage,
  ;; the hardest it had, each re-run from its own base and spec by the loop
  ;; alone. Argon coding with Sonnet testing passed every one first time, for
  ;; less money and time than this file's pair until then - Sonnet coding,
  ;; Flash testing - which passed them too, but retried on the hardest, mostly
  ;; on the Tester's own mistakes. Flash in this seat failed every packet, at
  ;; medium and at high effort: it worked in the REPL until its rounds ran out
  ;; and never wrote a file. One run per packet - a measurement to repeat when
  ;; a model in either seat changes. Sonnet 5.5 coded from 2026-10-02 until then.
  ;;
  ;; NOT THE SEAT'S FAMILY - the header's convention set aside on that
  ;; measurement. The verifiers still differ from it: the Tester is Anthropic,
  ;; the code reviewer and the security reviewer OpenAI.
  ;;
  ;; THE PROVIDER PIN IS THE POINT. Without :allow_fallbacks false the provider
  ;; you logged yesterday is not the one you get today, and a different provider
  ;; can mean a different chat template. One slug drew two different hosts
  ;; across three attempts of a single run, and "the model failed" was never a
  ;; safe reading after that.
  ;;
  ;; CACHING: Vertex caches a repeated prompt by itself (the measured runs'
  ;; records show the reads); on this shape nothing is asked for. No :pricing:
  ;; OpenRouter's reply carries the cost (row 129).
  :coder {:family :google
          :model "google/gemini-4-argon"
          :shape :openai
          :endpoint "https://openrouter.ai/api/v1"
          :key-env "OPENROUTER_API_KEY"
          :params {:reasoning_effort "medium"
                   :provider {:only ["google-vertex"] :allow_fallbacks false}}}

  ;; TESTER — Claude Sonnet 5.5 at medium effort, direct to Anthropic's API. A
  ;; dated selection of 2026-10-16 with the Coder's (above); gemini-3.8-flash
  ;; from 2026-09-16 until then. On the packets measured Sonnet tested in a few
  ;; rounds where Flash took many times as many; on the first KIT project's
  ;; first stage the Tester's own test was most often the first red gate.
  ;;
  ;; CHOOSE THE TESTER BY ITS TESTS, NOT ITS PRICE. The Tester writes MORE than
  ;; the Coder, from the contract alone, under more lint conventions, and its
  ;; mistakes are the ones nothing after it checks; the cheapest model in the
  ;; hardest seat is how this kit lost a whole run.
  ;;
  ;; THE MODEL NAME IS THE API'S, `claude-sonnet-5-5` - not OpenRouter's slug,
  ;; `anthropic/claude-sonnet-5.5` with a dot. No :provider pin: the endpoint
  ;; does no routing, so there is nothing to pin.
  ;;
  ;; CACHING ON THIS ROLE. On the :anthropic shape the adapter asks for
  ;; automatic prompt caching itself; a role that reads its prompt once says
  ;; `:cache_control nil` (the orchestrator and the Blueprint reviewer below).
  ;; The Tester's tool loop re-sends the whole conversation every completion,
  ;; so it is a role the cache is for: most of its prompt tokens are cache
  ;; reads, and a record's token lines show the share (`bb report`).
  ;;
  ;; :max_tokens IN :params, LIFTED OUT by the adapter to the request's top
  ;; level. Thinking counts against it, and the adapter's default of 4096
  ;; would cut a turn off mid-thought — 16000, the non-streaming ceiling the
  ;; API guidance gives.
  ;;
  ;; :pricing - USD per million tokens: base input, output, 5-minute and
  ;; 1-hour cache writes, cache reads. Input and output as `bb models` listed
  ;; them on 2026-10-16; the cache rates by Anthropic's multipliers (1.25x and
  ;; 2x the input for writes, 0.1x for reads).
  :tester {:family :anthropic
           ;; the rounds limit: the header says where 40 comes from
           :max-rounds 40
           :model "claude-sonnet-5-5"
           :shape :anthropic
           :endpoint "https://api.anthropic.com"
           :key-env "ANTHROPIC_API_KEY"
           :params {:output_config {:effort "medium"}
                    :max_tokens 16000}
           :pricing {:per-mtok {:in 2 :out 10
                                :cache-write-5m 2.5 :cache-write-1h 4
                                :cache-read 0.2}
                     :source "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing"
                     :as-of "2026-10-16"}}

  ;; CODE REVIEWER — gpt-6.1-sol at high effort, over OpenRouter's standard endpoint.
  ;; Dated selection 2026-10-07; gpt-5.6-sol from 2026-09-16 until then.
  ;;
  ;; WHY IT MOVED, MEASURED: `bb profile` said the line had two newer models at
  ;; the same price, and a bake-off read eight saved specs with both the old
  ;; reader and gpt-6.1-sol at high, a judge of another family reading blind.
  ;; The newer one raised half as many findings and the judge called nearly
  ;; all of them real; the old one raised twice as many and the judge called
  ;; fewer than half real, the rest being of the kind the method already
  ;; answers with its types rule. Each found a handful the other missed, so
  ;; neither wins on recall; for a list a person reads at a stop, fewer
  ;; findings that are mostly real is the better economy, and it read faster.
  ;; Measured as the spec reviewer; the three reading roles move together,
  ;; since they are one reader read three ways. The person decided on the
  ;; judge's reading. The records are the KIT session's working material, not
  ;; in the repository, so this is said in words.
  ;;
  ;; STANDARD, NOT FLEX. Flex was rate-limited upstream on most requests when
  ;; this was measured, and a verifier that fails for capacity rather than for
  ;; its findings is worth nothing. "openai" is also the provider's name, so the
  ;; pin is observed rather than guaranteed to exclude the flex and fast
  ;; endpoints; the report shows the service tier beside the provider, so a
  ;; routing change would be visible.
  ;;
  ;; HIGH, THOUGH MEDIUM MEASURED THE SAME on the defects that were tested. It
  ;; ships high by the user's decision, as insurance on defects unlike those:
  ;; the code reviewer runs once per unit of work and is the cheapest seat in the
  ;; loop.
  ;;
  ;; EFFORT IS A MEASURED SETTING, not a default — :reasoning_effort "high" on a
  ;; flash tier once produced minutes of thinking and an EMPTY final message
  ;; where the identical dispatch at medium answered quickly with real
  ;; findings. A flash tier spends a high budget thinking and has nothing left
  ;; to say with; this model showed no such shape at high.
  :reviewer {:family :openai
             :model "openai/gpt-6.1-sol"
             :shape :openai
             :endpoint "https://openrouter.ai/api/v1"
             :key-env "OPENROUTER_API_KEY"
             :params {:reasoning_effort "high"
                      :provider {:only ["openai"] :allow_fallbacks false}}}

  ;; PLAN REVIEWER — the spec reviewer's selection, block for block. Its own
  ;; role from 2026-10-06; before that `bb plan-review` borrowed the spec
  ;; reviewer's block. ONE CALL PER STAGE, IN ITS PLAN STEP, no tools: in
  ;; stage 0 the whole plan, after that the stage plan with the documents it
  ;; revised, read cold with method.md §03's checklist - a requirement written
  ;; for a stage not pulled, a risk with no owning stage, a lesson the new plan
  ;; does not answer, and the faults that recur in any plan.
  ;;
  ;; WHY THE SAME READER AS THE SPEC REVIEWER. The two readings share their two
  ;; properties: cold - the reader has seen nothing of the work and gets the
  ;; documents and the checklist in one call - and adversarial - asked for
  ;; faults, not confirmation. What differs is the checklist, which is text
  ;; sent with the call, not the kind of reader; so the reader that measured
  ;; best on contracts is the default here, and the cost is what it was when
  ;; the block was borrowed. A role of its own so that a project can set it
  ;; apart - a cheaper reader for the plan than for a contract, or a bake-off
  ;; on the plan alone - and so the profile says who reads the plan.
  ;;
  ;; NOT THE SEAT'S FAMILY, and `bb profile` checks it as it checks the spec
  ;; reviewer: the plan and every stage plan are the seat's writing.
  :plan-reviewer {:family :openai
                  :model "openai/gpt-6.1-sol"
                  :shape :openai
                  :endpoint "https://openrouter.ai/api/v1"
                  :key-env "OPENROUTER_API_KEY"
                  :params {:reasoning_effort "high"
                           :provider {:only ["openai"] :allow_fallbacks false}}}

  ;; SPEC REVIEWER — the same model as the code reviewer, over OpenRouter.
  ;; Dated selection 2026-09-18. ONE CALL PER SPEC, BEFORE START, no tools:
  ;; every place a target can be read two ways, every input the shapes allow
  ;; that no target mentions. `start` runs it for any spec without a current
  ;; review and stops for the Architect to read the list.
  ;;
  ;; NOT THE SEAT'S FAMILY, and `bb profile` checks it: the Architect who wrote
  ;; the spec sits in the seat, and a reviewer of its family reads a sentence
  ;; the way its author did. The spec's writer and its reviewer are different
  ;; families for the same reason the Tester is not the Coder's.
  ;;
  ;; WHY THIS MODEL, AND HIGH: measured. The line's first model, tried on specs
  ;; that had already been dispatched, named every ambiguity that had cost a
  ;; round; the Architect's own family at low effort matched it on the one spec
  ;; compared, but is the seat's family. On 2026-10-07 the line's newest,
  ;; gpt-6.1-sol, was read against it on eight saved specs with a judge of
  ;; another family: fewer findings, nearly all of them real by the judge's
  ;; reading, faster, same price (the code reviewer's comment has it). A cheaper
  ;; choice here is possible and not yet measured.
  :spec-reviewer {:family :openai
                  :model "openai/gpt-6.1-sol"
                  :shape :openai
                  :endpoint "https://openrouter.ai/api/v1"
                  :key-env "OPENROUTER_API_KEY"
                  :params {:reasoning_effort "high"
                           :provider {:only ["openai"] :allow_fallbacks false}}}

  ;; BLUEPRINT REVIEWER — Claude Opus 5.5 at high effort, direct to Anthropic's
  ;; API (the route since 2026-10-16). Dated selection 2026-09-25. ONE CALL
  ;; PER BLUEPRINT, BEFORE SIGN-OFF, no tools: `bb blueprint-review` sends the
  ;; stage document and the Blueprint with the method's two questions — is it
  ;; over-engineered (§07, the blueprint), and do its shapes and targets follow §06's
  ;; rules — and writes the findings beside the stage's other reviews. The spec
  ;; review still reads every packet cut from it, one at a time, before dispatch;
  ;; this reads the whole, for what a packet cannot show.
  ;;
  ;; THE SEAT'S FAMILY, AND THAT IS ACCEPTED — the second such exception, with
  ;; the Orchestrator, and unlike that one §05's reasoning DOES reach it: a
  ;; Blueprint is the seat's, as a spec is, and the spec reviewer is held to
  ;; another family for exactly that. It was measured instead of assumed. On
  ;; one build three families read the plan and both Blueprints cold, and this
  ;; model — the seat's own family — was the one that found the load-bearing
  ;; defect of each Blueprint round alone, twice: the route library's conflict
  ;; semantics, which would have cost a run each; and it did so at more real
  ;; findings per dollar than the reader of record. What the rule feared, a
  ;; reader that reads a sentence the way its author did, did not show on
  ;; Blueprints. `bb profile` does not check this role's family; `NOTES.md`'s
  ;; register watches it, and what would reverse the decision is a Blueprint
  ;; defect this reader read past that another family caught.
  ;;
  ;; HIGH, CHOSEN: the reads that measured it ran at high; the model's default
  ;; is medium and the two have not been compared here. `:cache_control nil`:
  ;; one completion, read once, so a cache write is a premium for nothing.
  ;; :pricing as the Tester's, at Opus's list prices.
  :blueprint-reviewer {:family :anthropic
                       :model "claude-opus-5-5"
                       :shape :anthropic
                       :endpoint "https://api.anthropic.com"
                       :key-env "ANTHROPIC_API_KEY"
                       :params {:output_config {:effort "high"}
                                :max_tokens 16000
                                :cache_control nil}
                       :pricing {:per-mtok {:in 4 :out 20
                                            :cache-write-5m 5 :cache-write-1h 8
                                            :cache-read 0.4}
                                 :source "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing"
                                 :as-of "2026-10-16"}}

  ;; SECURITY REVIEWER — gpt-6-astra at high effort, over OpenRouter pinned to OpenAI. Dated
  ;; selection 2026-10-08, by two bake-offs on the KIT's planted-fault fixture (`bb security-bake-off`):
  ;; once per stage, at its end, it reads the merged application with five tools in a sandbox with
  ;; no network, and a finding counts only when a test of its own fails. It found six and five of
  ;; six planted faults, and on the careful branch its only failing tests were two real flaws
  ;; nobody had planted; the other candidate found three in every run and never answered inside
  ;; its round limit. Fable 5.1 declined the review (its safeguard, three runs) and was
  ;; not a candidate after.
  ;;
  ;; ONE SECURITY REVIEWER FOR NOW, the person's decision: a second from another family may be
  ;; tried later for diversity. Tried 2026-10-16: Gemini 4 Argon found every planted fault, in
  ;; half the time and a fraction of the cost, but read the careful branch in a minute and missed
  ;; the real flaw Astra found there - and it is now the Coder's family, which rules it out here. It is a verifier - it reads what the Coder wrote to disagree
  ;; with it - so `bb profile` holds it to another family than the Coder's.
  ;;
  ;; WHAT IT COSTS: $0.46 to $1.50 a reading of the fixture, three to eight minutes of review;
  ;; a real stage is larger. Its reproduced findings go to triage (`bb security-route`), its hypotheses to the
  ;; person for the threat model.
  :security-reviewer {:family :openai
                      :model "openai/gpt-6-astra"
                      :shape :openai
                      :endpoint "https://openrouter.ai/api/v1"
                      :key-env "OPENROUTER_API_KEY"
                      :params {:reasoning_effort "high"
                               :provider {:only ["openai"] :allow_fallbacks false}}}

  ;; ORCHESTRATOR — Claude Opus 5.5 at medium effort, direct to Anthropic's
  ;; API, as the Tester is (the header has the route's date and what it gives up).
  ;; Dated selection 2026-10-02 (below); Fable 5.1 at low from 2026-09-16 until
  ;; then. TRIAGE ONLY: one
  ;; completion with no tools, on a red gate or a note, answering who owns it —
  ;; coder, tester, architect or human — with a reason and guidance for the
  ;; retried role. Dispatch itself is software (harness.loop.orchestrate); this
  ;; is the one judgement inside the loop that a mechanical proposal cannot make.
  ;;
  ;; IT SHARED THE CODER'S FAMILY until 2026-10-16, and since then shares the
  ;; Tester's; both are accepted, for one reason. §05's rule is for
  ;; verifiers: the Tester and the code reviewer exist to disagree with the Coder.
  ;; Triage neither writes code nor judges a diff; it reads a failure against
  ;; the contract and both roles' files. The decision (2026-09-16) is that
  ;; the family that reads a Clojure contract best should route, and the
  ;; safety is that an `architect` or `human` route stops the loop for a
  ;; person — a wrong route costs one paid attempt, not a merge. What is
  ;; watched: a triage that sides with one role's reading of the contract over
  ;; the other's where the other was right - the Tester's now, its family's.
  ;; `NOTES.md`'s register has the row.
  ;;
  ;; `:cache_control nil`, ON PURPOSE. A cache write costs a premium over
  ;; the input rate, and triage is one completion with no tools: the prompt
  ;; is read once and never again, so asking for the cache buys nothing and
  ;; pays the premium: cached triage cost more than uncached on the prompts
  ;; replayed. On this shape the adapter asks for the cache unless the role
  ;; says no, and this is the role saying no (row 25 (c)).
  ;;
  ;; LOW WAS MEASURED, ON FABLE — high until 2026-09-18, "because this is judgement work".
  ;; The judgement did not differ: the first adopter's triage prompts, re-asked
  ;; at low, blind, gave the same route on all but one uncontested call, the
  ;; same split on the contested one, and on the one disagreement the better
  ;; route under the rule that an ambiguous contract is the Architect's. The
  ;; saving is small: triage's cost is its prompt, and effort changes only what
  ;; the model writes, which here is one JSON block. :max_tokens as the Tester's.
  ;;
  ;; OPUS 5.5 AT MEDIUM, NOT THE CODER'S MODEL — a dated selection of 2026-10-02,
  ;; the person's decision on the first real project for its second stage.
  ;; Sixteen triage calls on Fable at low had routed fifteen right and one
  ;; wrong, so the judgement was not the reason; two things were. Price: Opus
  ;; lists at well under half of Fable, and triage pays for its prompt. And a
  ;; bias worth avoiding: triage judges the Coder's code against the Tester's
  ;; test, and a triage by the Coder's own MODEL - Sonnet was the Coder, and was
  ;; set aside for that - is the one form of kinship this role should not have;
  ;; the family rule (§05) is for verifiers and says nothing here, so this is
  ;; convention, like the Coder's family following the seat. Opus writes no
  ;; code in this profile, and since 2026-10-16 neither the Coder's model nor
  ;; the Tester's is Opus. The Blueprint reviewer is Opus too, reading the
  ;; Architect's prose: a kinship of family and not of model. The project
  ;; watched each route against what the fix turned out to be; `NOTES.md`
  ;; row 2 is where the KIT keeps that watch.
  :orchestrator {:family :anthropic
                 :model "claude-opus-5-5"
                 :shape :anthropic
                 :endpoint "https://api.anthropic.com"
                 :key-env "ANTHROPIC_API_KEY"
                 :params {:output_config {:effort "medium"}
                          :max_tokens 16000
                          :cache_control nil}
                 :pricing {:per-mtok {:in 4 :out 20
                                      :cache-write-5m 5 :cache-write-1h 8
                                      :cache-read 0.4}
                           :source "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing"
                           :as-of "2026-10-16"}}}}
